@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@
+# The Daily Grind — origin migration
+
+The installed web app is moving from **`https://www.thedailygrind.coffee`** (old)
+to **`https://app.thedailygrind.coffee`** (new). Origins live in
+`js/migration-config.js`; `npm run check` keeps the manifests consistent with it.
+
+## What carries over and how
+
+| Concern | Mechanism |
+|---|---|
+| **The installed app itself** | Old manifest declares `migrate_to` (new app id + `install_url`), new manifest declares `migrate_from` (old app id, `behavior: "suggest"`). Both sides must agree, so neither origin can claim the other on its own. Supporting browsers offer to replace the installed app in place. Switch `behavior` to `"force"` once you're ready to make it mandatory. |
+| **OS-level shortcuts** (dock/taskbar/home-screen jump lists) | Both manifests declare **identical** `shortcuts`, `scope`, `start_url`, `display`, `name`, so pinned icons and jump-list entries map 1:1. Shortcut URLs are `/?view=…` (not deep paths), so they resolve on any static host, before the service worker installs, and when forwarded from the old origin. |
+| **User configuration** (home store, usual drink, milk, size, theme, reminders → `localStorage["dg:prefs:v1"]`) | localStorage is per-origin, so it's handed off explicitly: **(1)** opening the old app sends the user to the new origin with a `#dg-handoff=…` fragment (never sent to servers; scrubbed from the URL with `history.replaceState`); **(2)** on first launch without prefs, the new app asks a hidden `migration-bridge.html` iframe on the old origin (works because the two are same-site; strict `origin`/`source`/nonce checks both ways). Imports are whitelist-sanitised and only applied if newer than what's already stored, so replays or crafted links can't clobber newer settings or inject data. |
+| **Stale caches on the old origin** | `legacy/sw.js` replaces the old service worker, purges its caches, and routes every navigation (including old deep links) to the handoff page, preserving `?view=`. |
+
+## Layout
+
+```
+index.html, manifest.webmanifest, sw.js, js/, icons/   → new origin (the app)
+legacy/                                                → old origin root ("we've moved" + bridge)
+js/prefs.js, js/migration-config.js                    → shared by both (copied by the build)
+```
+
+## Deploy
+
+```sh
+npm run build   # runs the consistency check, then writes:
+                #   dist/new-origin     → app.thedailygrind.coffee
+                #   dist/legacy-origin  → www.thedailygrind.coffee
+```
+
+1. Deploy `dist/new-origin` first and confirm it's reachable.
+2. Deploy `dist/legacy-origin` over the old site. Apply `legacy/_headers` (or the
+   equivalent on your host): the bridge must allow
+   `frame-ancestors https://app.thedailygrind.coffee`, and `sw.js` /
+   `manifest.webmanifest` should be `no-cache` so installed clients see the
+   `migrate_to` promptly.
+3. Keep the old origin serving these files for as long as installs may still
+   exist — it's what triggers migration and the settings handoff.
