@@ -0,0 +1,104 @@
+# Privacy by Design: The Daily Grind website
+
+These rules are part of the definition of done for any change to this site.
+
+## 1. Third-party audit
+
+Last audit: 2026-11-19. Method: listed every `src`, `href`, `url()`, `fetch` and `action` in
+the repo and checked them against the CSP.
+
+| Resource | Before | Now | Why it changed |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Hero photo | `images.unsplash.com` (CSS `url()`) | `/assets/img/hero-coffee.webp`, self-hosted | Every page view sent the visitor's IP, User-Agent and Referer to Unsplash. |
+| Fonts | System font stack | No change | No font CDN, so nothing leaks. |
+| Scripts | None | `/js/privacy.js`, first-party | |
+| Analytics / ads / social SDKs | None | None | Don't add any without a privacy review (see section 5). |
+
+**Public pages now make no third-party requests.** CSP `default-src 'none'` with only `'self'`
+sources, plus `Connection-Allowlist: (response-origin …)`, block anything new from loading
+until someone deliberately changes the policy.
+
+To re-audit: open DevTools, then the Network panel, filter on `-domain:<our-domain>`, and click
+through the site. The list should be empty. The DevTools Issues panel also reports CSP
+violations.
+
+## 2. Data minimisation (markup)
+
+- **Newsletter form** (`index.html`) asks only for an email address. No name, birthday, phone
+  number or location. The reason for asking is shown inline next to the field (`#newsletter-why`)
+  and links to the matching row in the privacy page. Nothing is pre-checked and there are no
+  bundled consents.
+- `autocomplete="email"` lets the browser autofill the field. Correction and capitalisation are
+  turned off for this identifier field.
+- A honeypot field is used instead of a third-party CAPTCHA, which would add a tracking
+  third party.
+- **Logout** is a `POST` form with a CSRF token, not a `GET` link, so prefetchers, crawlers, or
+  a cross-site `<img src="/logout">` can't trigger it.
+- All CSS and JS live in external files, so the CSP doesn't need `'unsafe-inline'`.
+
+## 3. Client-side data handling (`js/privacy.js`)
+
+- Never writes personal data to `localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, IndexedDB or cookies, and never
+  logs it.
+- Sends only `email` and `csrf_token` (not the whole form), same-origin, with `cache: 'no-store'`
+  and `referrerPolicy: 'no-referrer'`.
+- Clears the form as soon as the address has been handed off, and again on `pagehide`, so it
+  doesn't stay in the back/forward cache.
+- Error messages are generic and never repeat the address or server output.
+- Writes to the DOM only with `textContent`. Trusted Types are enforced with
+  `trusted-types 'none'`, so any `innerHTML`-style string sink will throw.
+- Detects features (`'globalPrivacyControl' in navigator`); it never sniffs the User-Agent or
+  fingerprints the device.
+- Honours **Global Privacy Control** and advertises support in `/.well-known/gpc.json`.
+
+## 4. Security and privacy headers
+
+These are defined in `_headers` (Netlify/Cloudflare Pages format; port them to your host if you
+deploy elsewhere). `<meta>` fallbacks for CSP and Referrer-Policy sit in each HTML `<head>`.
+`npm start` (statikk) sends the CSP header locally with `--csp`.
+
+| Header | Value (summary) | Purpose |
+|---|---|---|
+| `Content-Security-Policy` | `default-src 'none'`, `'self'` only, `form-action 'self'`, `frame-ancestors 'none'`, `base-uri 'none'`, `object-src 'none'`, Trusted Types, `report-to` | Stops XSS and blocks any request to an unvetted third party. |
+| `Connection-Allowlist` | `(response-origin "https://globalprivacycontrol.org")` | Network-layer allowlist that also covers prefetch, preconnect and WebRTC (Chrome 152+). CSP covers other browsers. Add every new outbound link origin here. |
+| `Reporting-Endpoints` | `csp="/api/reports/csp"` | The endpoint must strip query strings, IPs and emails before storing reports. |
+| `Referrer-Policy` | `strict-origin-when-cross-origin` | Paths and query strings never leak to other sites. |
+| `Permissions-Policy` | camera, mic, geolocation, sensors, payment, Topics/FLEDGE/attribution, etc. all `()` | Turns off features we don't use, including ad-tech APIs. |
+| `Strict-Transport-Security` | `max-age=300` to start | **Raise to `31536000; includeSubDomains; preload` once HTTPS is verified everywhere.** |
+| `X-Content-Type-Options` | `nosniff` | |
+| `X-Frame-Options` | `DENY` | Clickjacking protection for older browsers. |
+| `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy` | `same-origin-allow-popups` | Blocks cross-window XS-Leaks. |
+| `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy` | `same-origin` | Blocks cross-origin reads of our responses. |
+| `Cache-Control: no-store` | `/account*`, `/logout`, `/newsletter` | Pages with personal data are never cached. |
+| `Clear-Site-Data` | `"cookies", "storage", "cache"` on `/logout` | Logout leaves nothing behind in the browser. |
+
+### Requirements for the backend (outside this repo)
+
+- Session cookie: `__Host-session=…; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Path=/`. No other cookies.
+- `POST /logout`: validate the CSRF token, destroy the session server-side, send
+  `Clear-Site-Data`, then `303` redirect to `/`.
+- `POST /newsletter`: validate on the server (client validation is UX only), check the CSRF
+  token, rate-limit, use double opt-in, and support one-click unsubscribe that **deletes** the
+  address. Send no tracking pixels and no link-tracking redirects in emails.
+- Logs: truncate IPs, mask emails and tokens, keep for 30 days.
+- Fill in `csrf_token` server-side. The static markup leaves it empty.
+
+## 5. Checklist for new features
+
+1. Do we really need this data? Can we collect it at a coarser level (e.g. an age bracket
+   instead of a date of birth)?
+2. Is the reason shown inline, next to the field?
+3. Is the retention period defined, and is deletion automatic?
+4. Does it add a third party? If so: get a privacy review, self-host if possible, use a façade
+   (load only on click) for embeds, add SRI to versioned assets, add the origin to the narrowest
+   CSP directive and to `Connection-Allowlist`, and update the table in section 1 and on
+   `/privacy-policy/`.
+5. Does it need a powerful permission? If so, ask only after a user action and with an
+   explanation, and relax `Permissions-Policy` only for that feature.
+
+## Known limitations
+
+- statikk (local dev) can only set the CSP header and never serves dotfiles. That means
+  `/.well-known/gpc.json` and the other `_headers` entries only apply in production.
+- `<meta>` CSP can't express `frame-ancestors` or `report-to`, so those protections depend on
+  the HTTP headers.
