+
+      <article id="journal-text">
+        <p>Every bag of coffee we roast starts with a relationship, not a catalog. Long before a green bean ever reaches our roaster, someone on our team has walked the rows of the farm where it grew, shaken hands with the people who picked it, and tasted dozens of samples at a cupping table that is usually just a borrowed kitchen counter. We buy from roughly a dozen partner farms and cooperatives across Ethiopia, Colombia, Guatemala, Peru, and Sumatra, and most of them have been with us for five years or longer.</p>
+        <p>Our sourcing calendar follows the harvest, not the other way around. In the winter months we travel to Central America as the cherries ripen at altitude; in the spring we turn our attention to East Africa, where washing stations in Yirgacheffe and Guji are processing the season&rsquo;s first lots. During each visit we cup blind, scoring every sample for sweetness, acidity, body, and clarity. Only coffees that score above 85 on the Specialty Coffee Association scale make it onto our shortlist, and even then we roast small test batches back home before committing to a full container.</p>
+        <p>Price is where we try hardest to do right. We pay a minimum of 40 percent above the Fairtrade floor price for every lot we buy, and we publish those numbers on our website so anyone can check our math. For many of our partners we also agree on prices before harvest, which gives growers the security to invest in better drying beds, new fermentation tanks, or simply a raise for their pickers. When a farm hits a rough year because of weather or disease, we keep buying, because a relationship that disappears the moment it becomes inconvenient is not much of a relationship at all.</p>
